  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "ucB3VFuJdGYPjWdC1+GXJj91nxg=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T07:34:30.071Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "This is named as **the biggest operational sharp edge of the MVP**, in the architecture document and again in the repo's `CLAUDE.md` so it is not forgotten.\n\n`hash $doc_id consistent` is Ketama-style consistent hashing across the `server` entries, so:\n\n- **adding** a backend remaps ~1/N of documents \u2014 the same fundamental tradeoff any stateless scheme has \u2014 and until `switchboard` supports migration this is a coordinated maintenance event, not a self-serve operation;\n- **removing** a backend makes the documents pinned to it **unroutable**, under the same rule.\n\nThe long-term fix is acknowledged and deferred: an explicit `doc_id \u2192 backend` **directory** (Redis or Postgres) so adding a backend does not remap anything. It requires coordinated migration support in `switchboard`, which does not exist \u2014 recorded as open question 4, \"how do operators migrate a document between backends?\", cross-cutting `switchboard` and `reactor`, to be decided *before operating this at any meaningful scale*.\n\nReload itself is safe and uses nginx's own mechanics: master parses the new config and old workers keep running if it fails; on success new workers start, old workers finish in-flight requests and exit, zero dropped connections. `scripts/reload.sh` wraps `nginx -t` then `nginx -s reload` and fails loudly if validation fails. A long-lived WebSocket survives a reload because old workers drain gracefully \u2014 proven by an integration test that opens a real WS, reloads mid-flight and asserts both the connection and a frame round-trip survive.\n\n## Provenance\n- `apps/switchboard-lb/ARCHITECTURE.md` \u2014 \u00a76.3 Adding / removing backends; \u00a75.4 External directory (deferred); \u00a76.2 Reload semantics; \u00a79 Q3 and Q4\n\n_Extracted from the Powerhouse repository at **v6.2.2-dev.74**._",
        "updatedAt": "2026-09-03T07:34:30.071Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T07:34:30.071Z"
    }
  },
  {
